# Pin tool versions so every developer and CI runs identical toolchains.
# Bump intentionally after vetting the upstream changelog; do not let
# the stdlib (or any other tool below) drift under the project via `latest`.
#
# Go 1.26.8 is the current pin (`task vuln-check` clean as of 2026-09-03).
# 1.26.5, the prior pin, carried five stdlib vulnerabilities this code
# reaches, all fixed in 1.26.6 (GO-2026-6218 quadratic net/url.resolvePath;
# GO-2026-6090 unbounded post-handshake messages in crypto/tls; GO-2026-6089
# ReadHeaderTimeout on the net/http h2c check; GO-2026-5972 encoding/asn1
# recursion depth; GO-2026-5026 net/http idna Punycode labels); the pin
# takes the newest patch release so the next advisory is less likely to
# land on a pin that is already behind. Earlier floors: 1.26.5 added the
# crypto/tls GO-2026-5856 ECH fix; 1.26.2 carried GO-2026-4971 and
# GO-2026-4918; 1.26.1 carried six (5 in crypto/x509, 1 in html/template).
# Re-run `task vuln-check` after every Go bump.
go 1.26.8
goreleaser 2.17.0
cosign 3.1.2
shellcheck 0.11.0
syft 1.49.0
caddy 2.11.4
# go-task (the `task` runner). Pinned so CI installs it via mise and runs the
# exact same Taskfile tasks developers run locally (`task lint`, `task gosec`,
# `task vuln-check`, ...). This makes .tool-versions the single source of truth
# for every tool version in both places: CI no longer `go install`s any linter
# at `@latest`, so a fresh tool release can't break CI on unchanged code and a
# local run can never disagree with CI on tool versions.
aqua:go-task/task 3.52.0
# Go quality and vulnerability tooling, run by `task lint` (staticcheck +
# gosec) and `task vuln-check` (govulncheck) both locally AND in CI (CI invokes
# these same tasks — see .github/workflows/ci.yml). Pinning via mise — rather
# than `go install ...@latest` in CI — keeps them reproducible across machines,
# survives Go toolchain bumps (a `go install` binary lives in the active Go's
# bin dir and is shadowed the moment Go's version moves), and stops a new lint
# rule in a fresh gosec/staticcheck release from breaking CI on unchanged code.
# staticcheck and gosec install as aqua-verified prebuilt binaries; govulncheck
# has no aqua prebuilt and compiles from source via the `go:` backend. Pinning
# govulncheck does NOT reduce CVE coverage: it queries the live vuln.go.dev
# database at run time, so newly-published CVEs are still caught. Bump these
# intentionally — re-run `task lint && task vuln-check` after each bump.
staticcheck 2026.1
aqua:securego/gosec 2.28.0
go:golang.org/x/vuln/cmd/govulncheck 1.6.0
